      <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-extrabold text-slate-900 tracking-tight">
        LIC Riders Decoded: Accidental Death &amp; Disability vs Term Assurance Rider — Cost, Cover &amp; Who Should Take Which
      </h1>
      <p class="text-base text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
        An agent says “rider le lo, sirf thoda extra premium hai” — and it usually is cheap. The trap is not the price, it is the trigger. One LIC rider pays only when an accident causes death or total permanent disability; the other pays on death from any covered cause, but its cover is capped at ₹25 lakh and it can only be added when the base policy starts. Buy the wrong trigger and your family gets nothing extra at exactly the moment they expected it. This guide separates the two riders LIC buyers are most often sold, with their published limits, sample costs and exclusions — and the branch quotation is final for your policy.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Quick Summary Box -->
    <div class="bg-brand-50 border border-brand-200 rounded-2xl p-6 space-y-3">
      <h2 class="text-sm font-bold text-brand-900">Riders in one view</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-sm text-brand-800 leading-relaxed">
        <li><strong>A rider is an add-on, never a standalone policy.</strong> It lives and dies with the base policy: if the base lapses or goes paid-up, the rider benefit ceases too. Neither rider below pays anything on survival or maturity.</li>
        <li><strong>Accidental Death and Disability Benefit Rider (ADDB, UIN 512B209V02):</strong> extra cover only for accidents. Accidental death (within 180 days of the accident) pays the Accident Benefit Sum Assured; total permanent disability pays the same sum in monthly instalments over 10 years, and future premiums are waived to the extent of that sum.</li>
        <li><strong>New Term Assurance Rider (TAR, UIN 512B210V02):</strong> extra life cover for death from any covered cause during the rider term. Entry 18–60, cover to age 75, term 5–35 years — but the rider sum assured is capped at <strong>₹25 lakh across all LIC policies</strong> and cannot exceed the base sum assured.</li>
        <li><strong>The one-line difference:</strong> ADDB answers “what if an accident disables or kills me?” TAR answers “what if I die at all, before my savings plan has built up?” A family that needs ₹1 crore of life cover cannot get it from a ₹25 lakh rider — that needs a real term plan (see our <a href="/guides/lic-term-plans-head-to-head/" class="text-brand-700 font-semibold hover:text-brand-800">term plans head-to-head guide</a>).</li>
        <li><strong>Illustrative cost:</strong> ADDB at about ₹1 per ₹1,000 of cover per year (regular premium) means ₹10 lakh of accident cover costs roughly ₹1,000 a year; a 30-year-old paying the published sample rate of ₹2.44 per ₹1,000 for a 20-year Term Assurance Rider pays roughly ₹6,100 a year for ₹25 lakh of extra death cover. Both figures are illustrative — the branch quotation is the only figure LIC will charge.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <!-- Guide Content -->
    <div class="prose prose-slate max-w-none text-sm leading-relaxed space-y-6 text-slate-700">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">1. What a Rider Is — and What It Is Not</h2>
      <p>
        A rider is a clause attached to a base policy (an endowment, money-back or other non-linked plan that allows riders) for an additional premium, paid in the same mode as the base premium. It cannot be bought on its own, it has no surrender value and no paid-up value of its own, and it does not change the base policy's maturity value. Its only job is to change what is paid on one specific event.
      </p>
      <p>
        That dependence cuts both ways. Because the rider premium is small, agents bundle riders casually — and because the rider is small print, buyers discover the trigger only at claim time. Before comparing prices, compare triggers: <em>which event, within which deadline, and with which exclusions</em>. The rest of this guide does exactly that for the two riders.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">2. Accidental Death and Disability Benefit Rider (ADDB): The Accident-Only Rider</h2>
      <p>
        ADDB (UIN 512B209V02) is a non-linked rider available with regular, limited and single-premium plans that allow it. “Accident” here has a strict meaning in the rider document: a sudden, unforeseen, involuntary event caused by external, visible and violent means. A heart attack, a diagnosed illness, or a death months after an accident is not an ADDB claim, even if the base policy pays its own death benefit.
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2">
        <li><strong>Accidental death:</strong> if the life assured dies as a result of the accident within <strong>180 days</strong> of the accident date, the nominee receives the Accident Benefit Sum Assured (ABSA) <em>in addition to</em> whatever the base policy pays. Death after 180 days does not trigger this rider.</li>
        <li><strong>Total permanent disability:</strong> if the accident causes total and permanent disability (as defined in the rider document) within 180 days, the ABSA is paid in <strong>equal monthly instalments over 10 years</strong>, and future premiums — for the base policy as well as the rider — are waived to the extent of the ABSA. If the base policy matures or becomes a death claim before all instalments are paid, the remaining instalments are paid out with that claim.</li>
        <li><strong>Limits:</strong> minimum ABSA ₹10,000; the ABSA cannot exceed the base policy sum assured, and total accident-benefit cover across LIC policies is generally capped at <strong>₹1 crore</strong> (₹2 crore if you hold a Jeevan Shiromani plan with the required minimum cover). Entry age 18 completed; cover runs to the policy anniversary nearest age 70, or the end of the base term, whichever is earlier.</li>
        <li><strong>When it can be added:</strong> unlike the Term Assurance Rider, ADDB can in many cases be added during the premium-paying term, subject to at least 5 years of the base term remaining — confirm this for your plan at the branch, because plan-level rider availability varies.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        <strong>Exclusions to read before signing:</strong> intentional self-injury or suicide, insanity, influence of intoxicants or narcotics (unless medically prescribed), riots, war, hunting, mountaineering and adventurous sports, and accidents while engaged in armed-forces or police duty unless that cover was specifically opted for (police-duty cover carries a higher premium rate). These exclusions apply to the rider only — the base policy's own terms govern its own benefit.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">3. New Term Assurance Rider (TAR): Extra Death Cover, Capped and Inception-Only</h2>
      <p>
        The New Term Assurance Rider (UIN 512B210V02) is the simpler trigger: if the life assured dies during the rider term, the Term Assurance Rider Sum Assured is payable, whatever the cause of death (subject to the base policy's and rider's standard exclusions, such as the suicide clause in the early years). It is, in effect, a small term plan bolted onto a savings plan.
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2">
        <li><strong>What it pays:</strong> the rider sum assured on death during the term. On survival to the end of the rider term, nothing is payable — there is no maturity value, no surrender value and no paid-up value.</li>
        <li><strong>Limits:</strong> minimum rider sum assured ₹1 lakh; maximum <strong>₹25 lakh in total across all LIC policies</strong> of the life assured, and never more than the base policy's sum assured. Entry age 18–60 (nearest birthday), cover ceasing age 75, rider term 5–35 years (10 years minimum under limited-premium payment).</li>
        <li><strong>When it can be added:</strong> only with non-linked plans, and <strong>only at the inception of the base policy</strong>. If your policy is already running, you cannot add TAR to it later — that is the single most common disappointment with this rider, and the reason to decide on it before the base policy is issued.</li>
        <li><strong>Revival:</strong> a lapsed rider can be revived together with the base policy within the revival window, on satisfactory evidence of insurability and payment of arrears with interest — the same revival route as the base policy (see our <a href="/guides/lic-policy-revival/" class="text-brand-700 font-semibold hover:text-brand-800">revival guide</a>).</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">4. Head-to-Head: ADDB vs Term Assurance Rider</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto not-prose">
        <table class="w-full text-sm border border-slate-200 rounded-xl overflow-hidden">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-slate-50 text-left">
              <th class="border border-slate-200 px-3 py-2">Question</th>
              <th class="border border-slate-200 px-3 py-2">ADDB Rider</th>
              <th class="border border-slate-200 px-3 py-2">Term Assurance Rider</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td class="border border-slate-200 px-3 py-2 font-semibold">Pays on</td>
              <td class="border border-slate-200 px-3 py-2">Accidental death within 180 days, or total permanent disability from an accident</td>
              <td class="border border-slate-200 px-3 py-2">Death from any covered cause during the rider term</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
