            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="border border-slate-200 px-3 py-2 font-semibold">Maximum cover</td>
              <td class="border border-slate-200 px-3 py-2">Base sum assured, aggregate ₹1 crore (₹2 crore with Jeevan Shiromani)</td>
              <td class="border border-slate-200 px-3 py-2">₹25 lakh across all LIC policies, and never above the base sum assured</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="border border-slate-200 px-3 py-2 font-semibold">Entry / cover to</td>
              <td class="border border-slate-200 px-3 py-2">Entry 18+; cover to age 70 (nearest birthday)</td>
              <td class="border border-slate-200 px-3 py-2">Entry 18–60; cover to age 75 (nearest birthday)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="border border-slate-200 px-3 py-2 font-semibold">Can be added later?</td>
              <td class="border border-slate-200 px-3 py-2">In many cases yes, during the premium-paying term (at least 5 years remaining) — branch to confirm</td>
              <td class="border border-slate-200 px-3 py-2">No — only at inception of a non-linked base policy</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="border border-slate-200 px-3 py-2 font-semibold">Maturity / surrender value</td>
              <td class="border border-slate-200 px-3 py-2">None</td>
              <td class="border border-slate-200 px-3 py-2">None</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="border border-slate-200 px-3 py-2 font-semibold">If base lapses</td>
              <td class="border border-slate-200 px-3 py-2">Rider benefit ceases with the base policy</td>
              <td class="border border-slate-200 px-3 py-2">Rider benefit ceases with the base policy</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">5. Illustrative Cost Maths — Why Both Riders Look Cheap</h2>
      <p>
        Both riders are priced per ₹1,000 of rider sum assured, so the maths is simple — and deliberately labelled illustrative, because the exact rate depends on age, term, premium-paying term and the plan's current brochure. The branch quotation is final.
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2">
        <li><strong>ADDB, illustrative:</strong> at a published sample rate of about ₹1 per ₹1,000 of Accident Benefit Sum Assured per year for regular-premium plans, ₹10 lakh of accident cover costs roughly <strong>₹1,000 a year</strong>, and ₹25 lakh costs roughly ₹2,500 a year. Police-duty cover costs more. Single-premium and limited-premium rates are higher per year because the payment window is shorter.</li>
        <li><strong>Term Assurance Rider, illustrative:</strong> published sample rates per ₹1,000 per year for regular premium include ₹2.44 for a 30-year-old on a 20-year term and ₹5.52 for a 40-year-old on the same term. So ₹25 lakh of extra death cover costs roughly <strong>₹6,100 a year at 30</strong> (2,500 × ₹2.44) and roughly <strong>₹13,800 a year at 40</strong> (2,500 × ₹5.52), before any taxes shown on the quotation.</li>
        <li><strong>Combined example, illustrative:</strong> take a base policy with a ₹25 lakh sum assured, plus ADDB of ₹25 lakh, plus TAR of ₹25 lakh. On accidental death, the family receives the base policy's own death benefit <em>plus</em> ₹25 lakh (ADDB) <em>plus</em> ₹25 lakh (TAR) — ₹50 lakh of rider money on top of the base. On natural death, the family receives the base benefit plus ₹25 lakh (TAR only) — ADDB pays nothing. On total permanent disability from an accident, the life assured receives ₹25 lakh in monthly instalments over 10 years (about ₹20,833 a month) with future premiums waived to that extent, and TAR pays nothing.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The combined example is the whole point: the same ₹25 lakh + ₹25 lakh of rider cover produces three completely different outcomes depending on <em>how</em> the event happens. Check your family's gap against the worst outcome, not the best one.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">6. Who Should Take Which — A Decision Framework</h2>
      <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-2">
        <li><strong>Young family, single earner, savings plan already chosen:</strong> TAR first, if the policy is still at inception stage. It is the only one of the two that covers the most likely tragedy — death from illness — and ₹25 lakh extra for roughly ₹6,100 a year at 30 is genuinely cheap. But treat it as a top-up: work out how much cover you actually need with our <a href="/guides/lic-term-cover-how-much/" class="text-brand-700 font-semibold hover:text-brand-800">how-much-cover guide</a>, and buy a standalone term plan for the rest.</li>
        <li><strong>High accident exposure (long daily commute, two-wheeler, field or site work):</strong> ADDB earns its place, because it is the only one of the two that pays anything for disability — and disability, not death, is the risk that stops income while expenses continue. The 10-year instalment structure is a feature here, not a flaw: it replaces income month by month.</li>
        <li><strong>Already hold a pure term plan for the full cover amount:</strong> TAR adds little — your term plan already pays on death from any covered cause, with no ₹25 lakh cap. ADDB may still add value for the disability case, which a basic term plan does not cover.</li>
        <li><strong>Policy already running, no riders attached:</strong> TAR is gone — it was inception-only. ADDB may still be addable (section 2); anything else needs a new policy or a separate term plan, not a rider request at the branch counter.</li>
        <li><strong>On a tight budget:</strong> skip both before you skip the base premium. A lapsed base policy kills every rider attached to it, and a rider has never rescued a family whose main policy lapsed for non-payment.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">7. Five Traps That Make a Rider Worthless</h2>
      <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-2">
        <li><strong>Assuming ADDB covers all deaths.</strong> It does not. A death from illness on a policy with only ADDB attached pays the base benefit and nothing extra — the most expensive misunderstanding in this guide.</li>
        <li><strong>Assuming ₹25 lakh of TAR is “enough cover”.</strong> It is a top-up, capped by design. A family needing ₹1 crore that stops at TAR is under-insured by ₹75 lakh.</li>
        <li><strong>Missing the 180-day ADDB clock.</strong> Complications, coma or a death more than 180 days after the accident fall outside the rider, however genuine the link. Keep accident records, FIR/medical papers and dates from day one.</li>
        <li><strong>Buying two accident riders as if they stack automatically.</strong> Current plan brochures list accidental-death/disability and accident-benefit riders as alternatives for the same policy, not automatically cumulative cover — check the brochure for your plan before paying two rider premiums for one accident.</li>
        <li><strong>Letting the base policy lapse.</strong> Rider cover ceases when the base policy lapses or goes paid-up. Revival must cover the base and the rider together, within the revival window.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">8. The Other LIC Riders, in One Paragraph Each</h2>
      <p>
        Two more riders appear on current plan brochures and are worth knowing by name, though neither is the subject of this guide: the <strong>Critical Illness Rider</strong> pays a lump sum on diagnosis of a listed critical illness (cancer, heart attack, stroke and others as defined in its document, with its own waiting periods and exclusions) — it answers a risk neither ADDB nor TAR touches. The <strong>Premium Waiver Benefit Rider</strong> waives future base premiums if the life assured suffers a covered disability or critical illness, keeping the base policy alive when income stops. Both are plan-dependent, both carry their own definitions and exclusions, and both should be compared on their trigger lists, not on their brochures' headlines.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">9. Checklist Before You Sign for a Rider</h2>
      <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-2">
        <li>Write down the trigger in one sentence (“pays ₹X if Y happens before Z”) and check it against the rider document, not the agent's summary.</li>
        <li>Confirm the rider sum assured, the aggregate cap across all your LIC policies, and whether the rider can be added later or only at inception.</li>
        <li>Get the rider premium shown separately on the written quotation — not bundled into a single “total premium” figure — and confirm the taxes shown on that quotation.</li>
        <li>Check exclusions that apply to you personally: occupation (police/armed forces), hazardous hobbies, and any pre-existing condition the rider document carves out.</li>
        <li>Keep the rider endorsement with the policy bond. At claim time, the nominee files under the base policy and the rider together — a missing endorsement is a delayed claim.</li>
      </ol>

      <p class="text-xs text-slate-500 border-t border-slate-200 pt-4">
        This guide is independent educational content, not LIC advice. Rider UINs, limits, sample rates and exclusions are as published in rider documents and plan brochures at the time of writing and may change; the servicing branch's written quotation and your policy's rider endorsement are final for your case. Worked examples are illustrative — the branch's quotation is the only figure LIC will charge.
      </p>
    </div>

